WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.000
Hello. I'm Briana, and in this video,

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.300
I'm gonna go over a lot of things having to do with Bash,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.420
mostly the basics.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.700
We're going to start off really basic, like, what is Bash?

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.420
What is that, you know--how do I even get around?

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.040
All the way through configuration, using different types of scripts with Bash.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.500
So hopefully at the end, you'll feel a bit more comfortable working in the terminal,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.260
So starting off: as you can see, Bash is a terminal.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.800
It is the terminal used on Mac and Linux by default,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.920
so it's a little bit different than PowerShell.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.800
When people say terminal, it could mean a lot of different things.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.160
It could mean Bash.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.080
It could mean something like zsh shell, which is kind of similar,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.960
or PowerShell, but this is all about Bash.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.500
So we open up Bash.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.040
I opened up my terminal application on my Mac computer,

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.480
and the very first thing that I want to type--

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.860
so it's kind of intimidating if you've never been here before--

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.940
but I want to just do "echo $0",

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.440
so echo space dollar sign zero.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.240
This--I click enter, return.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.300
This says Bash,

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.160
So this is just confirming that I am in Bash.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.180
So find your terminal, type "echo $0".

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.200
If it says Bash, then you're in the right spot.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.880
Whatever I type today should work for you as well

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.720
unless you have some different configuration setup.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.820
So we're here.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.620
We don't know what "here" is other than Bash,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.600
so I'm going to type pwd.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.220
So pwd stands for Print Working Directory.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.960
And that just means it's going to print out wherever I currently am.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.580
So currently, I'm in my computer's users directory and then githubteacher.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.320
So that's the user that I'm logged into right now.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.920
I can use pwd at any time to know where I am,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.980
which is pretty helpful.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.040
The next command I want to show is called ls.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.320
So ls is going to list whatever's in my current directory,

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.040
if there's any files or other directories.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.220
So here, I see I've got locations, documents, library.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.260
What you'd expect.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.920
We also have ls space dash al.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.420
Let's see if there's any hidden files here.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.600
And it also gives us some more information.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.180
We see all these letters on the left. We'll talk about those later.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.500
Owner of the file, their permission level,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.800
and then here the dot in front of a file means it's a hidden file,

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.540
so when I just did ls,

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:23.480
I wasn't able to see those files like .trash, .atom, .bash_history, which we'll talk about soon.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.560
So know that ls and ls -al are there

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.920
so that you can see what's going on in the directory that you're currently in.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.420
When I list my files and directories,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.300
I see documents, downloads, all these things.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.780
I want to make a new folder, a new directory called Projects,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.740
so I'm gonna do mkdir for make directory

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.160
and then space and then Projects.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.620
I'll do a capital P here.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.560
So I type "mkdir Projects", hit enter.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.540
It tells me nothing,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.480
But if I do ls...

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.700
I can see, now I have...

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.660
a Projects directory that I didn't have before.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:05.920
So I just created an empty file, that's it...

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.580
using mkdir.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.500
So I've made this directory, but I'm not currently in it.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.100
So, right, if I do pwd, I'm still in my githubteacher directory.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.920
So to change directories,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.520
I'll do "cd Projects".

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.040
cd space Projects

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.340
What you see on the left-hand side changes a little bit.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.340
That'll depend on what configurations,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.980
what, you know...

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.840
That can look different from computer to computer,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.920
especially with developers that have been using Bash for a while.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.880
If I do pwd now...

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.380
It's a little different.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.020
So before, I was just in githubteacher;

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.480
now, I'm in githubteacher/Projects.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.580
So I created the directory and then changed directory into it.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.280
If I do ls here,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.580
we see nothing.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.460
ls -al.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.380
I just see these dots, which means I can go back.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.040
So we've changed directory into a directory,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.100
but, like, maybe I want to go back.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:02.140
Let's say I want to go back to being in this githubteacher directory.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.560
I can type--

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.360
Come back, terminal.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.860
I'll type cd space dot dot.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.640
Now if I do pwd,

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.660
I'm back in githubteacher.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.680
No big deal.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.560
We've created a directory.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.760
It's good to know how to get rid of a directory just in case.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.800
So if you need to delete a directory,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.640
you can do rmdir.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.520
So mkdir to create.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.320
"rmdir", and then I'll do Projects.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.060
The one I just created, I'm just going to scrap it.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.300
Now if I do ls--oops not ld.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.860
If I do ls...

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.860
then Projects is no longer there.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.560
Even if I do ls -al, it's not there anymore.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.580
I'll go ahead and recreate that.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.640
"mkdir Projects" just so that we have something to--

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.120
we're going to keep making some changes.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.660
But now you've seen a lot of the basics.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.240
We know how to navigate around.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.160
We know where we are with pwd.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.580
We can change directories with cd,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.620
both forwards and backwards;

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.200
list the files in any given directory; make directories; delete directories.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.280
It's already a lot, right, with just a few really basic commands.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:07.680
Hopefully by now,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.200
you already are figuring out there's nothing to be afraid of

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.600
when you're in the terminal.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.380
Let's talk a little bit about working with files.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.880
We've talked about directories; let's work with files.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.140
All files are case-sensitive with Bash.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.480
This is important to consider, as you might be working with something--

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.900
another program, like, maybe you're working with Git.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:31.460
and the way Git or any program handles case sensitivity

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.400
might be different than how Bash handles case sensitivity,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.340
So keep in mind: Bash is case-sensitive.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.460
Let's cd into that Projects--change directories into Projects.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.220
And I'm gonna make a new file.

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.520
Because we do ls, we see there's nothing.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.460
To create a new empty file,

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.340
I'm just going to type touch--

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.620
I'll call it file1.md

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.000
for markdown, the file format that I use a lot.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Press return. Tells me nothing.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.540
If I do ls,

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:02.060
now I see I have this file1.md.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.500
If I do ls -al,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.200
I've still got those dot files.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:07.540
We have file1.md.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:08.740
See who it's owned by,

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.620
and the letters are a little different, but we'll get there.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.620
Creating a file, pretty easy.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.780
To delete that file, rm--

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.180
just "rm file1.md".

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.060
Now it's no longer there.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.460
Some different ways we can manipulate files.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.340
Let's go ahead and make this file again.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.560
We'll call it file2.md.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.240
I've created this file.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.160
Let's say I want to copy this file,

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:33.720
so I want to make a duplicate.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.480
I'm going to type "cp file2.md"

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.500
and then the name that I want the new copy to have.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.520
So I'm going to call this file2-copy.md.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.540
As soon as I do that, if I do ls,

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:50.580
I see I've got these two files here.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.140
They're empty. They're both empty,

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.140
but if file2 had some contents in it,

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.900
that would now be mapped exactly to file2-copy.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.760
You can also rename files just really simply.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.780
So if I do mv...

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:06.000
Let's say, I want file2-copy, this new file,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.000
I want that to just be called file3,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.660
so I'll do mv file2-copy.md--the old name--

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.360
and then the new name.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.300
I'm going to call file3.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:20.120
If I do ls -al, file2-copy's now been renamed file3.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:21.640
Pretty straightforward.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.820
So far since we've started working with files,

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.500
we've used touch, rm, mv, and cp.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:29.300
So touch creates a file,

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.700
rm removes it,

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.940
cp copies it and creates another one,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:37.980
and mv just renames it--or moves it to a different file name.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.060
We also have cat for concatenate.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:44.740
Concatenate.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:47.660
So this is a way to create single or multiple files.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:49.580
You can view the contents of a file;

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.360
you can link files together,

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.180
so view multiple files;

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:55.000
redirect output in terminable files.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.960
There's a lot you can do with cat.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.680
Since these two files are empty right now,

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.000
let's put some--let's put some things in there.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.080
So let's do that with VI.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.540
So VI is kind of a different level.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.820
So Bash and terminal can already be intimidating.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.940
VI, I would say, is another level.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:16.460
VI is always a text editor within the terminal.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:18.560
You might hear people also--VIM.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.800
VIM and VI are different.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.540
I don't use either one regularly,

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:24.580
so I'm sure there are a lot of people out there

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.380
who can go into a lot more detail than I can,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:29.220
but it's important to know a little bit about VI.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.100
You might end up in VI by accident,

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:35.660
and it doesn't behave, necessarily, how you'd think.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:38.700
So let's--let's use it.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.920
To use VI, let's add some contents to file2.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.880
So I'm gonna type "vi file2.md".

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.020
It's gonna open up file2 in VI.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:50.040
So already it looks very different.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:54.140
If I tried to just type anything, it might not work.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:58.240
Once you're in VI mode, to actually insert,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.780
you press i or j.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.560
So there's two modes of VI:

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.480
there's insert and command.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.120
So I'm going to type i--

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.240
I hit i on my keyboard,

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.300
and now we can see at the bottom, it says insert.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.640
Now I'm able to type.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:15.440
So I'm going to say:"File 2 is the best file in the entire directory!"

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.420
So I've made my changes.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.000
Now what do I want to do, right?

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.340
How do I get out? If I press enter, it just goes down.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.540
Getting--I think that's like--

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:29.060
"How do I get out of VI" is the most asked question on Stack Overflow.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:32.200
So here we are. How do we get out of VI?

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.740
First, we need to get into that command mode, right?

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.360
So I'm going to press ESC.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.240
We see the insert is gone at the bottom.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.240
I'm going to shift so it's COLON,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.100
And now we can see that at the bottom.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.420
That's putting us into command mode.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:49.580
"w" for write because I want to save these changes.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:51.360
And "q" for quit.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.340
I could just do q, and it wouldn't save,

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.180
but wq will save and quit.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.000
Hit enter.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.180
So that's it. I'm out of VI.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.760
Let's do that again.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:06.980
Let's do it for file3, so I'm going to open "vi file3.md".

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.680
First thing I do is...

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.440
type i to begin insert mode.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.360
Start typing. "File 3 is a pretty good file too."

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.460
Now, my instinct as somebody who uses a computer

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.620
is to do CMD s or CTRL s to save,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:27.400
but that will not work in VI the same way it will work in other programs.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:31.240
To get out of this, I'm gonna hit ESC to get out of insert mode...

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.700
colon wq...

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.420
and enter. We're done.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.860
So we've kind of done these basics of VI.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:41.960
Just the need-to-know basics of how to get out,

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:44.100
and how to get in, how to make some changes.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:47.020
Let's use that cat command.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:50.380
So you're gonna do "cat file2.md",

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:53.060
and it prints out just the contents of file2

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.520
that I just put in there.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:58.200
I can do "cat file2.md file3.md".

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.460
So I'm doing two different files, and it prints out both...

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:05.780
both of the contents. The contents for both of the files.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.700
So, so far, we've done a lot, right?

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.420
We've worked with directories, how to get around.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:13.080
If I do pwd, the first thing we really talked about--

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.040
I'm still in my Projects directory--

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.000
ls. I still have these two files here: file2 and file3.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.240
Let's talk a little bit about how to configure your Bash shell.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.540
So what that means is right now my Bash is pretty...

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.220
monotone.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.800
There's just white and black. Um...

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:34.820
If you're ever--if you ever see somebody else working in a terminal,

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.820
whether it's on a video or in person, you know,

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:38.820
sitting next to somebody on an airplane,

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:40.660
their terminal might not look like this.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:42.660
It probably doesn't look like this.

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.460
I configure mine to have some different colors

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.200
that make it easier for me to read,

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.300
which is some, you know--the most basic, probably, way to do it.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:54.420
But you can configure your Bash terminal for a lot of different things.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.600
So you can include aliases so that I--

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.460
you only have to type something small

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:01.160
but actually will run a much longer script or command.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:04.600
You can configure authentication tokens

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.600
for different services like Git and GitHub.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:11.600
Let's start out with the colors.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:13.640
So for the colors...

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.580
The first thing we're gonna look for is this .bash_profile and .bashrc.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.800
So again pwd. I'm in Projects.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:24.820
I need to cd back into my main user directory.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.340
And I'm going to do ls -al.

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.000
So I see I have...

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.180
bash _history and bash_sessions,

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:37.020
but since I haven't used the bashrc or bash_profile file yet,

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:39.360
they are not on this user--

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.540
They are not on this user account.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:46.300
If those files exist or not by default will depend on your operating system.

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:49.020
So Linux, you probably already have those.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:51.260
Mac, you might not.That's OK.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.260
We're just going to create them.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:55.040
So we know how to create files:

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:59.260
"touch .bashrc"

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:03.540
and "touch .bash_profile".

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.660
bash_profile is executed for login shells,

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:13.040
and bashrc is executed for interactive non login shells

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:18.580
meaning bash_profile is going to have those authentication tokens that we talked about;

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:21.260
whereas, bashrc is gonna have more just general...

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.840
general configurations in it.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:27.140
Okay, so I'm gonna open up that bashrc.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:28.520
I'm not gonna do it in VIM.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:32.860
I'm actually going to use my favorite text editor, which is Atom.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:37.100
And to do that, I'm gonna go to Atom...

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.180
There is a way to configure Atom, so it's a--

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.960
you just Atom space and the file name.

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.680
I haven't done that yet. Let's see if it'll work just by default.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.380
Atom space dot. Oh, great. So Atom space...

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:50.940
anything will open up that specific file.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.000
Dot in Bash, like I just did,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.020
means current directory.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.000
So this opened the current directory

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.540
so everything I see on the left-hand side here

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:02.940
is the same I would see in an ls -al.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.300
I'm going to open up this bashrc file in Atom,

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:08.460
and I'm going to copy this--

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.120
which I'll put in the video description here--

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:13.400
here.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:14.800
Nope, actually, no, I'm not.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.500
I'm going to put that in the bash_profile.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.560
So what this does is...

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:24.860
This is making sure that we only are sourcing the bashrc from the bash_profile.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.320
If Bash doesn't know where to look,

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.700
it might not behave how you'd expect it to.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:31.400
You might get some different errors and bugs.

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:32.980
So this is making sure that, um,

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.960
the bash_profile is always looking for the bashrc

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:39.900
to make sure it's taking whatever configurations are there into account also.

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.280
So I'll save that change in my bash_profile.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:46.840
Now when I log into any machine from a console,

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:49.480
this bashrc is automatically going to be called.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:53.900
Let's go ahead and configure some colors.

00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:56.300
Let me pull up these...

00:14:56.380 --> 00:14:58.920
different colors here.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.120
This is what I'm looking at.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.900
So I'll put a link to this little gist of code.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:06.700
So it's a little bit different if--

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:08.420
depending on your operating system.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:11.700
Since I'm on a Mac, I'm gonna add this to the bash_profile.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.440
So here's this script.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.540
So this is going to be affecting my Git output specifically.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:18.940
So you could do that or this one here.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:23.380
This is specifically for Git.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:27.640
Copied one. I'll put that in bash_profile.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:31.400
This--this hash sign I'm putting here is comment.

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:34.380
Colors for Git output.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:36.620
And I'll paste that in and save.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:39.240
And I also want to...

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:45.300
So this is something I got from a friend.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:46.940
I'll save that, get rid of that,

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:51.060
and then back in the bashrc, let's write colors for other text.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.180
Let's try this.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:56.180
Now here, let's open up terminal again.

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:00.500
There we go. Okay, so...

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.640
CLICOLOR=1 enables coloring of your terminal,

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:08.900
and then the LSCOLORS specifies how to color specific items.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:09.800
Okay.

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:11.779
There we go.

00:16:11.779 --> 00:16:14.600
So there's a lot of different ways you can...

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:16.220
you know...

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:18.880
customize this so that the output is much different.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.220
Here we do pwd. It's still black. ls- al.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:26.260
Here we see how, you know,

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.020
certain things look different

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.000
based on if they're a directory or if they are just a file.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:35.320
Customizing your prompt and configuring things

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.280
goes a lot deeper than this.

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:40.380
You could--what you can do is absolutely massive.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.080
But the most important thing to know

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:47.500
is that you find those customizations in your .bashrc and your .bash_profile.

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:51.500
Okay. Let's move on to something else.

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.560
Let's talk about the internet.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.340
So I'm gonna type clear here.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:58.220
Clear is just a terminal--

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:03.660
a shell Bash command to literally clear off everything that's on the terminal

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.640
so that you have a fresh start. Clear. All right.

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:11.540
So you can interact with the internet and other networks from Bash.

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:14.360
That means, you know, whatever program you're using

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:16.060
can interact with the internet.

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:20.600
You'd usually want to do this to check the network connectivity or speed.

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:24.200
So if I type "ping google.com",

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:28.500
that tells me how fast my internet connection is really quickly.

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:33.640
I'm gonna press CTRL--hold it down and then click c to exit that.

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:34.760
So if you ever stuck in a mode--

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:36.520
it's either VI mode, right--

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:39.620
and then you would do escape colon wq

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:43.280
or something like this where there's an operation running--a system task--

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:46.800
then I would do CTRL + c to exit that.

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:49.820
So interacting with the internet:

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:52.720
a lot of different CLI tools will access the internet.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:54.600
Git is a great example of this.

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:58.520
So I'd have my remote repository on github.com,

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:01.180
my local repository on my computer.

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.650
I can interact with that entirely from Bash,

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.260
and that will be done via the Internet.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:10.100
So just remember anything that you do, um...

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.020
in the shell, right--

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:15.120
you are not automatically just on your own computer.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:19.480
Which is a great thing, but also good to know in case you might be making changes to something?

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:23.420
Let's move on to: What else can you do from Bash?

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:26.260
so we've done quite a bit already, right?

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:28.200
We've created files. We've navigated around.

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:30.340
We know how to get out of V and get--

00:18:30.340 --> 00:18:32.220
or get out of VI and get into VI.

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:35.600
Let's talk about how to do some more advanced things,

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:37.400
mostly scripts.

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:39.020
So as an example,

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.780
I would like to show you...

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:47.220
this script, and we're gonna create it, we're gonna run it, we're gonna make it work.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.080
So this script here...

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:52.560
This example is what I use before I teach class--

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:53.700
that I was talking about.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:55.700
It quits certain applications; it hides my dock.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:59.660
Because I really don't want slack notifications coming up while I'm teaching a class.

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:02.860
And it opens certain applications.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:04.720
So Mouspose and Moon,

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:07.200
which are things I use to show my mouse and move windows around.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:08.600
Make sure those are open.

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:10.400
Make sure certain browsers are open.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:13.520
And it even triggers other scripts like the teach-class script.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:17.460
One that we're going to create is really simple.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.540
So here we see the top line is:

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:22.960
this #!/bin/bash .

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:26.000
That just tells Bash that this file is actually a Bash script.

00:19:26.049 --> 00:19:28.559
So I'm gonna go ahead and go back to Bash here.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.720
I'm going to cd out of this repository,

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.720
so I'm back in Projects.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:34.400
We can see that here.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:35.500
I'm going to--

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:37.340
Let's take a look at this script.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.340
Okay, so it's just a good-morning world.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:40.660
So I'm going to touch good-morning.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.060
So I just created a file called good-morning.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:47.120
And I'm going to "vi good-morning",

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:49.120
so now I'm in VI, okay?

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:51.860
Next, let's insert.

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:54.500
IfI type i so that we're in insert mode.

00:19:54.580 --> 00:20:00.800
And at the top it's that #!/bin/bash .

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:02.360
Enter. Clear.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.360
We want to just clear the prompt, right?

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:06.360
Like you could do as any standalone command.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:07.800
And echo,

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.340
which we haven't talked about yet.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:11.400
"echo" just has the terminal--

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.580
has Bash print out exactly what's in quotes.

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:18.840
so echo "Good morning, world."

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.740
will then have the terminal print that out.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:26.440
"echo" is a really, really useful command when you're working with shell scripts

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:29.160
because frequently through your scripts you might want to know,

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:30.720
you know, like a console log of kind of thing,

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:32.180
you know, what's going on.

00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:35.800
I want to have this as feedback. This message will print out...

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:38.580
at a certain time during my script.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.900
So that's good. Looks good.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:45.100
I hit escape colon wq

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:46.100
and exit.

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:49.380
Now let's see the moment of truth.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:50.700
We've created this script.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:52.420
If I just type "good-morning",

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:54.000
let's see what--what we expect.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.420
It says "command not found".

00:20:56.420 --> 00:21:01.640
Let's talk a little bit about all these letters at the beginning.

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:03.900
So back in the terminal here,

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:07.620
if I do "ls- al" we see at the beginning of these files...

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:11.600
all these drwxr, you know, slash.

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:15.740
Those are impacting our ability to run that script out of the command.

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:20.220
So chmod and file folder permissions.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:24.140
So this first blank indicates the file type,

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:28.300
whether it's a directory with a d or just a slash--it's a regular file.

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:31.300
From then, we have these three other groups:

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:35.800
one for the owner, members of the group, and all other users.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:39.000
So that means any given file might have  different permissions

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:41.160
for other people on the computer.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.160
You know, as an owner, I might be able to do something,

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:45.000
but then if somebody else logged into the machine,

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:48.060
they would only be able to, you know, read that file.

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:50.120
They couldn't execute whatever that script was.

00:21:51.460 --> 00:21:53.120
So what you want to do--

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:57.100
if we take a look at our newly created script, which is called good-morning,

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:58.800
nowhere is it executable.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:02.040
We have r, read access; w, write access for the owner,

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:03.600
so I'm allowed to make edits

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:05.620
No one else can make edits even.

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:07.840
But we want to have x.

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:11.800
I'm not so concerned of if other users have any access to this file.

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.900
I just want to have access right now.

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.420
So what I'm gonna do is type:

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:21.640
"chmod +x" and then the filename.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:26.320
So if I do ls- al, now I see good-morning has...

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:27.400
this x next to it.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:29.760
Now it's red because it's an executable script

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:31.000
Instead of just good-morning,

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:36.440
we're going to do dot, you know, period slash good-morning.

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:39.200
And it cleared the terminal and did "Good morning, world."

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:42.070
Earlier, it wasn't working because I was just typing "good-morning",

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:45.080
and remember when we're navigating throughout the terminal,

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:48.300
it is relative and it matters where we are.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:51.300
So it didn't know exactly where to look for good-morning here,

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.320
but when we do dot slash,

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:56.780
meaning go back directory and look for the slash,

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:59.760
then good-morning--or current directory and slash,

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:00.780
then we're in good shape

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.440
Then it knows exactly where to look.

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:05.500
So obviously, this is a very simple script example,

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:07.380
but that's how easy it is.

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:10.620
Anything that you run in any command

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:12.760
can be put inside of a shell script,

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:15.980
whether it's basic shell, you know, language things

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:19.300
or API calls or other programs.

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:21.960
So a lot to know here.

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:23.580
There's a lot to--to be done.

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:24.900
And thank you.

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.840
Hopefully you feel better about using Bash.

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.840
Hopefully you feel like you can, um,

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:32.240
not be so afraid of using your terminal.

